1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
The Vancouver 2010 Olympic Winter Games are the ultimate showcase for peak performances on ice and snow.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
But the amazing athletes are only part of the story.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:19,000
The games are also packed with the latest technology, cutting-edge engineering, and state-of-the-art venues.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
The 2010 Winter Games are one of the most tech-driven sporting events ever.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:31,220
Take a sneak peek into what the world will see when the torch is lit.

6
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:34,040
It's winter tech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:52,580
For sports fans around the globe, February 12, 2010 will be the start of the most anticipated

8
00:00:52,581 --> 00:00:54,400
two and a half weeks in winter sports.

9
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:57,920
But for host country Canada,

10
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:01,780
the Olympic countdown clock started ticking in July of 2003.

11
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:06,620
The 21st Olympic Winter Games in 2010

12
00:01:06,621 --> 00:01:10,620
are awarded to the city of Vancouver.

13
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:13,400
The games are coming back to Canada.

14
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:18,100
It's the start of an enormous challenge

15
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:19,580
for Vancouver, British Columbia.

16
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:25,680
It's like putting on three Super Bowls a day for 17 straight days

17
00:01:25,681 --> 00:01:28,020
because you have so many different competition venues.

18
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:34,640
Preparing venues for 86 medal events involves high-flying engineering,

19
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:40,340
sub-zero construction, and inspired design.

20
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:44,240
But all of this takes serious real estate.

21
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:49,340
The Olympic footprint is vast, stretching nearly 100 miles,

22
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:53,960
Vancouver serves as the central hub with competitions in the city of Richmond to

23
00:01:53,961 --> 00:01:59,760
the south and Whistler to the north. One of the key ingredients for any winter

24
00:01:59,761 --> 00:02:06,160
games is snow and mountains of it. Just two hours north of Vancouver are the

25
00:02:06,161 --> 00:02:11,820
peaks of Whistler and Blackcomb, famous for their world-class slopes. They'll host

26
00:02:11,821 --> 00:02:19,080
36 medal events including cross-country skiing, sliding sports, and a first

27
00:02:19,081 --> 00:02:26,840
appearance from a new Olympic event ski cross inspired by motocross ski cross

28
00:02:26,841 --> 00:02:33,800
is a fast-paced rugged race with moguls jumps and wipeouts

29
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:44,200
prepping these runs and making sure the snow is ready takes serious machinery

30
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:48,700
meet the snow cat

31
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:53,620
Tipping the scales at nearly 14 tons, this is the ultimate all-terrain vehicle.

32
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:02,920
A 460-horsepower diesel engine and hefty winch system allow the Cat to handle inclines up to 50 degrees.

33
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:08,100
The Snowcat is at home in extreme conditions.

34
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,340
Steel tracks give it grip on icy surfaces.

35
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:17,360
A monster plow muscles through even the highest snow drifts.

36
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:24,280
Even the wiper blades are specially designed, heated with engine coolant to melt through tough storms.

37
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:30,920
And for a 14-ton machine, this cat is light on its feet.

38
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:36,200
Its perfect weight distribution keeps the snow cat from sinking into deep snow.

39
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:44,520
Whistler snow groomers plow through over 200 runs a day as they constantly rebuild the crowded slopes.

40
00:03:44,521 --> 00:03:51,100
As the skiers travel down, they generally move the snow downhill, creating thin spots at the top.

41
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:54,900
So a large part of the grooming operation is getting that snow back uphill.

42
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:56,514
There's quite a lot to it.

43
00:03:56,515 --> 00:03:59,300
It's more than just pushing a lawnmower along the grass for sure.

44
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,660
Snowcats do more than simply move snow.

45
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:05,700
They change it on a molecular level.

46
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:13,740
The way the snowcats operate, the tiller on the back is a rotary shaft that spins at a high rate of speed.

47
00:04:13,741 --> 00:04:16,280
It's driven hydraulically by motors and pumps.

48
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:20,460
That actually breaks down the snow crystals and introduces heat into it.

49
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:25,900
As the cat passes by, it'll freeze and it'll make it a harder surface.

50
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:31,140
Whistler is more than doubling its fleet of snow cats for the 2010 Winter Games.

51
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,780
With the Olympics, we're looking at an additional 40 cats in the valley.

52
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,220
So it's going to be quite a lot of iron crawling around these mountains.

53
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:41,580
Of course, snow cats are useless,

54
00:04:41,581 --> 00:04:44,400
unless the mountains have plenty of the white stuff to shape.

55
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:50,220
The trails at both peaks are ringed by a network of 300 snowblowers

56
00:04:50,221 --> 00:04:55,000
that turn 39 million gallons of water into a perfect blanket of snow.

57
00:04:56,280 --> 00:05:00,360
Right here, we're looking at the top-of-the-line snowmaking automated machine.

58
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:01,400
It's a fan gun.

59
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:02,920
We can take this gun.

60
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:04,620
We can bury anything in sight.

61
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:05,900
We can bury the trees.

62
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:07,160
We can bury this lift hut.

63
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:09,800
We can cover the chairs on the chairlift with snow.

64
00:05:09,801 --> 00:05:14,520
Whistler's snow guns could fill an NHL hockey rink to the top of the glass in

65
00:05:14,521 --> 00:05:21,860
mere hours. Fed by insulated underground pipes, snow guns work by infusing water

66
00:05:21,861 --> 00:05:27,020
with compressed air, breaking it down into tiny droplets. The fans blow these

67
00:05:27,021 --> 00:05:32,760
droplets into the air where they turn into snow before hitting the ground.

68
00:05:32,761 --> 00:05:37,660
It combines the latest in technology, the weather gauge, automatic water

69
00:05:37,661 --> 00:05:42,220
modulation so as the temperature gets colder it automatically turns itself on

70
00:05:42,221 --> 00:05:47,800
and increases the water output to maximize temperature windows. The natural

71
00:05:47,801 --> 00:05:51,780
snow is very light it's great to ski on but it goes away very quickly so this

72
00:05:51,781 --> 00:05:57,760
beefs it up a little bit it makes it more resilient. Snowmakers can adjust the

73
00:05:57,761 --> 00:06:03,760
guns to make any kind of snow from soft recreational powder to race quality

74
00:06:03,761 --> 00:06:10,960
bulletproof ice. With 36 medal events on Whistler's slopes, the snowmaking team

75
00:06:10,961 --> 00:06:14,380
needs to constantly coordinate its efforts with the groomers and Whistler's

76
00:06:14,381 --> 00:06:20,180
weather station. Worldwide Olympic partner Samsung has provided the Olympic

77
00:06:20,181 --> 00:06:25,200
workforce with smartphones equipped with a unique tool called Wireless Olympic

78
00:06:25,201 --> 00:06:31,480
Works or WOW. Come game time, the operating center will transmit critical

79
00:06:31,481 --> 00:06:34,480
information around the clock.

80
00:06:34,481 --> 00:06:36,880
Updates about weather or other information

81
00:06:36,881 --> 00:06:39,920
will be accessible at the touch of a button.

82
00:06:39,921 --> 00:06:41,960
The special software also allows workers

83
00:06:41,961 --> 00:06:44,680
to send urgent group messages to teams of athletes

84
00:06:44,681 --> 00:06:46,220
or Olympic staff.

85
00:06:46,221 --> 00:06:49,540
If there's a storm coming in and the team lead

86
00:06:49,541 --> 00:06:52,980
needs to let everybody know about the weather conditions,

87
00:06:52,981 --> 00:06:57,340
they would be able to use WOW to very quickly communicate

88
00:06:57,341 --> 00:07:00,480
to hundreds of people with just one click of a button.

89
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:06,480
With the snow teams armed with real-time communication and the slopes freshly groomed,

90
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:10,480
alpine skiers are able to rocket down the mountain.

91
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:16,480
Racing at speeds of 80 miles per hour, they're constantly flirting with disaster.

92
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:21,480
In a sport where less than a second can be the difference between first place and last,

93
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:25,480
making minor adjustments to their technique can have a huge impact.

94
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:30,480
But trying out new moves while careening down a run is not the best testing ground.

95
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:35,720
That's where the high-tech wind tunnel comes in.

96
00:07:35,721 --> 00:07:40,780
At the CalSpan University of Buffalo Research Center,

97
00:07:40,781 --> 00:07:44,060
Olympic hopefuls train in wind tunnels run by the same scientists

98
00:07:44,061 --> 00:07:46,500
who help ensure the safety of NASA's space missions.

99
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:52,540
In downhill skiing, as racers strive for speed,

100
00:07:52,541 --> 00:07:54,440
air resistance plays a big role.

101
00:07:54,441 --> 00:08:04,520
Air resistance, or drag, is measured in pounds.

102
00:08:04,521 --> 00:08:09,960
Every pound of drag skiers shave off helps them go faster.

103
00:08:09,961 --> 00:08:15,500
In downhill skiing, you get speeds up to 80 miles an hour, and at those speeds, typically

104
00:08:15,501 --> 00:08:21,560
the drag on your body is at the order of 30 to 40 pounds during the whole race.

105
00:08:21,561 --> 00:08:24,460
dominates your ability to win or lose.

106
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:28,120
Try to get into your best low tuck at this point.

107
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:31,580
Mike Holden holds a PhD in hypersonic aerodynamics,

108
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:33,740
but he has another claim to fame.

109
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:37,140
His wind tunnel training has produced a dozen Olympians

110
00:08:37,141 --> 00:08:39,000
who went on to win gold.

111
00:08:39,001 --> 00:08:42,080
... forward and then backward.

112
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:45,100
Holden is constantly pushing the skiers

113
00:08:45,101 --> 00:08:46,380
to lower their drag number.

114
00:08:46,381 --> 00:08:57,740
basically what's holding you back from going the fastest you can is the aerodynamic drag

115
00:08:57,741 --> 00:09:06,940
and we can easily improve the top by four or five pounds downhill racers also need maneuverability

116
00:09:06,941 --> 00:09:13,340
so the skiers train in tackling turns with streamlined precision bring the hips up a little

117
00:09:13,341 --> 00:09:16,840
In the higher positions, like the turning positions,

118
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:20,440
we can literally shave 10 pounds off,

119
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:24,480
which turns into an order of tenths of seconds

120
00:09:24,481 --> 00:09:26,080
by the time you get to the bottom.

121
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:30,700
I've actually lost a race by a hundredth of a second overall.

122
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:34,560
To us, a tenth of a second to shave that off of our time.

123
00:09:34,561 --> 00:09:39,800
That would be for a runner shaving a minute off of their 10-mile run.

124
00:09:39,801 --> 00:09:46,460
Racing down an alpine course at 80 miles per hour also requires strength and toughness.

125
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:50,340
One major thing they have to have is physical training

126
00:09:50,341 --> 00:09:54,540
to be strong enough to be able to take the forces in skiing.

127
00:09:55,460 --> 00:09:59,200
For athletes who do well in the wind tunnel, the training Holden offers is invaluable.

128
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:04,780
I'm really excited. It was a great opportunity to be involved in the wind tunnel,

129
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:07,000
and I'll take so much back to the hill.

130
00:10:07,001 --> 00:10:11,680
It's fun, really, and it's really great to help athletes.

131
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,780
While good snow is key to many winter sports,

132
00:10:17,240 --> 00:10:19,520
it can also be a dangerous adversary.

133
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:27,260
I got her, I got her.

134
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:32,400
Whistler's peaks have over 200 known avalanche hazard zones.

135
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:36,040
One sits right on the Olympic men's downhill course.

136
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
A slide path is a known avalanche path.

137
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:42,980
There are several slide paths that are close to the Olympic venue,

138
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:44,400
so we have to monitor them closely.

139
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:50,100
Avalanches are a serious hazard, causing hundreds of deaths a year worldwide.

140
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:56,740
To prevent disaster, Whistler has one of the largest avalanche control programs in the world,

141
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:00,440
combining high-tech tools and old-fashioned fieldwork.

142
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:06,880
The weather station coordinates with up to 20 field teams to identify potential avalanche hazards.

143
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,700
There's been a lot of snow just over there. It's a bit of a hazard.

144
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:16,920
When needed, highly trained two-person teams use explosives to trigger controlled avalanches.

145
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:24,720
Aside from monitoring the snowpack on a daily basis, we go out and we'll test the snow with explosives.

146
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:30,000
A small avalanche now can help prevent a bigger one later.

147
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:34,520
We have various means at our disposal for testing the snow stability.

148
00:11:34,521 --> 00:11:36,120
One of them is an avalancher.

149
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:41,480
This is propelled to the avalanche start zone with an avalancher gun that uses compressed nitrogen.

150
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,720
Once this projectile is delivered to the snow, it detonates on impact,

151
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:48,980
and that tests the stability of the snow.

152
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:54,280
We also can deploy explosives from a helicopter,

153
00:11:54,281 --> 00:11:58,720
or we'll take the lift up to the top of the mountain and toss the hand charges onto the slope.

154
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:03,480
The charges are one kilogram emulsion explosives, which are highly water resistant.

155
00:12:03,481 --> 00:12:08,140
sometimes we'll have nothing but a hole in the snow which usually indicates that

156
00:12:08,141 --> 00:12:14,420
the snow is stable but a lot of times it triggers an avalanche at the 2010 winter

157
00:12:14,421 --> 00:12:20,420
games the avalanche prevention team will be on constant guard while the weather

158
00:12:20,421 --> 00:12:24,980
team keeps the peak safe athletes across the continent prep for the wildest ride

159
00:12:24,981 --> 00:12:32,120
on the mountain courtesy of an intensely engineered sled and the fastest

160
00:12:32,121 --> 00:12:36,760
shoot of ice in the world that's coming up on modern marvels

161
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:44,920
at the 1960 olympic winter games in squaw valley california officials were unsure if a skier had

162
00:12:44,921 --> 00:12:49,640
missed a gate in the men's slalom they asked a broadcaster if they could review the videotape

163
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:51,880
and the instant replay was born

164
00:12:51,881 --> 00:13:04,380
As Canada prepares for the Vancouver 2010 Olympic Winter Games, Olympic organizers use

165
00:13:04,381 --> 00:13:08,520
a lot of high-tech tools.

166
00:13:08,521 --> 00:13:19,260
From a massive network of snowmakers to 14-ton snowcats that groom the snow.

167
00:13:19,261 --> 00:13:25,860
avalanche prevention teams who keep the fluffy white stuff from turning deadly.

168
00:13:28,860 --> 00:13:33,540
While the peaks at Whistler get an Olympic makeover, athletes across the

169
00:13:33,541 --> 00:13:39,840
continent prepare to go for gold. At the US Olympic training complex in Lake

170
00:13:39,841 --> 00:13:46,160
Placid, New York, technology meets tradition to build the modern Olympian.

171
00:13:49,260 --> 00:13:53,640
One event combines high speeds, athleticism,

172
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:57,380
and one intensely engineered piece of equipment,

173
00:13:58,380 --> 00:14:01,280
bobsled.

174
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:05,620
Well, the sport is definitely for speed junkies,

175
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:06,780
adrenaline junkies.

176
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:11,860
That's why the auto racing guys love it so much.

177
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:18,360
And every racer needs a fast car.

178
00:14:19,260 --> 00:14:21,319
Bobsleds are very much like race cars.

179
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:25,112
There's a tremendous amount of aerodynamics, importance on suspension.

180
00:14:25,113 --> 00:14:27,280
These are no-engine, all-speed vehicles.

181
00:14:30,260 --> 00:14:33,720
Bobsledding is an event as old as the Olympic Winter Games themselves.

182
00:14:35,300 --> 00:14:39,420
And early sleds were a far cry from the sleek design seen today.

183
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:49,080
The bobsled evolved over the decades, getting lighter as weight restrictions force designers to think in terms of aerodynamics.

184
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:57,660
a real turning point came in 1992 when the bodine bobsled project was created

185
00:14:57,661 --> 00:15:03,320
it uses race car technology to create more aerodynamic sleds

186
00:15:03,321 --> 00:15:10,940
they design sleds exclusively for the u.s team

187
00:15:10,941 --> 00:15:16,800
Each sled is crafted out of a lightweight metal frame,

188
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:20,560
wrapped in fiberglass or a composite material.

189
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:23,980
Add axles and two sets of runners,

190
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:26,660
and you have a high-octane speed machine.

191
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:30,740
But the most critical feature is the split-hull design,

192
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:33,300
which allows the sleds to not just turn,

193
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:38,720
but twist, conforming to each corkscrew bend in a sliding track.

194
00:15:38,721 --> 00:15:42,380
Probably the most important thing is this split here in the sled.

195
00:15:42,381 --> 00:15:44,760
This is called the articulation of the sled.

196
00:15:44,761 --> 00:15:49,280
This is the way the sled walks on and off the curve.

197
00:15:49,281 --> 00:15:50,920
This is where all the money is.

198
00:15:50,921 --> 00:15:59,740
The articulation in the sled sometimes are engineered to a one ten-thousandth of an inch.

199
00:15:59,741 --> 00:16:04,300
These advancements in sled design have revolutionized the sport.

200
00:16:04,301 --> 00:16:11,840
And the Bodine sled transformed the U.S. team into medal contenders.

201
00:16:11,841 --> 00:16:13,528
The re-engineered sled helped U.S.

202
00:16:13,529 --> 00:16:16,160
bobsledding break a nearly 50-year drought in winning

203
00:16:16,161 --> 00:16:19,300
Olympic medals.

204
00:16:19,301 --> 00:16:23,760
They hope the sleds will bring them gold and whistler.

205
00:16:23,761 --> 00:16:28,380
Of course, engineering is useless unless it's paired with ability.

206
00:16:28,381 --> 00:16:31,740
It finally comes down to the talent of the driver.

207
00:16:31,741 --> 00:16:34,600
The best piece of equipment on the hill doesn't mean that you're going to win.

208
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:39,540
You've got to have the equipment, the driver, the team, the pit crew.

209
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:41,200
There's a lot of variables that add up to winning.

210
00:16:45,900 --> 00:16:51,960
Once launched, bobsledders travel so fast, they withstand forces up to four Gs.

211
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:56,320
Greater than astronauts in a space shuttle launch.

212
00:16:56,321 --> 00:17:04,720
You go through some of the high pressure turns and you're being sucked right down into the sled and you're trying to fight against it.

213
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:10,360
You have to fight against it in order to stay up and be able to concentrate on the track, concentrate on your driving.

214
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:12,940
It's just a rush, really.

215
00:17:16,680 --> 00:17:24,280
But before any athletes can compete for titles, Olympic venues have to be built from the ground up.

216
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:29,520
And venue designers are just as competitive as the athletes.

217
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:33,720
We've heard from all the teams now it is the fastest track in the world,

218
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:37,080
bar none, they say, and probably one of the most technically challenging.

219
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:39,920
As far as we're concerned, it's the best in the world.

220
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:48,080
How did engineers build the fastest bobsled course in the world?

221
00:17:50,720 --> 00:17:54,740
In the early days of bobsledding, the sliding track was a simple chute

222
00:17:54,741 --> 00:17:57,060
carved into a hillside and filled with ice.

223
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:05,420
But now, its anatomy is as much concrete and steel as ice and snow.

224
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:14,420
Well, this is a mock-up that will show you the design of how a track is built

225
00:18:14,421 --> 00:18:17,240
and what the inner workings of a track is like.

226
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:20,120
You'll notice the exterior layer is the concrete.

227
00:18:20,900 --> 00:18:24,540
Behind the concrete, we have rebar for reinforcement, of course,

228
00:18:24,541 --> 00:18:26,800
just like if you were to build a foundation for a building.

229
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:30,360
And beneath the ice lies the surprising key ingredient

230
00:18:30,361 --> 00:18:33,720
of any sliding track, the refrigeration system.

231
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:38,420
Even in winter, sliding tracks need constant cooling

232
00:18:38,421 --> 00:18:39,900
to maintain an icy surface.

233
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:43,980
Underneath the track, there's ammonia pipes

234
00:18:43,981 --> 00:18:46,340
that are embedded in the concrete surface itself,

235
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:49,060
and ammonia is pumped from the refrigeration plant.

236
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:51,220
That's what keeps the concrete surface cold,

237
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:53,280
and then that's what gives us a good running surface

238
00:18:53,281 --> 00:18:53,981
that they have here.

239
00:18:54,540 --> 00:19:01,860
The cooling system is made of a million pounds of piping, stretching 71 miles with 800 bends.

240
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:09,480
Putting it together requires 27,000 precise welds.

241
00:19:11,320 --> 00:19:15,880
But doing all that work on top of a freezing mountain was a daunting prospect.

242
00:19:16,540 --> 00:19:23,280
Previous bobsled tracks were all done and welded piece by piece in the field.

243
00:19:23,281 --> 00:19:26,480
and consumed a lot of time and money.

244
00:19:27,380 --> 00:19:31,320
You're working in elevations that are upwards of 3,000 meters,

245
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:36,520
and you're faced with everything from grizzly bears to snow,

246
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:40,080
so you want to get all of those elements out and into the shop.

247
00:19:43,500 --> 00:19:45,780
Engineers came up with a unique alternative.

248
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:49,500
They welded most of the track before it even got to the mountain.

249
00:19:49,501 --> 00:19:55,280
We looked at all of that cost and decided to build it in the shop.

250
00:19:55,540 --> 00:19:59,300
So we built segments, designed them to be truck size,

251
00:19:59,460 --> 00:20:05,000
and we trucked those segments up to the job site and minimized the man hours on site.

252
00:20:06,780 --> 00:20:10,040
Building a bobsled track may be a monster construction effort,

253
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:13,180
but it's the minute details that make it ready for competition.

254
00:20:13,920 --> 00:20:16,680
These tracks are built within the millimeter of the design,

255
00:20:16,681 --> 00:20:22,240
And similar to building a skyscraper, the vertical portion and the exits of the curves

256
00:20:22,241 --> 00:20:26,300
all have to be in a tolerance of millimeters.

257
00:20:26,301 --> 00:20:30,600
Once fully assembled, the track is carefully coated with ice up to two inches thick to

258
00:20:30,601 --> 00:20:32,720
provide a uniform sliding surface.

259
00:20:32,721 --> 00:20:37,200
It takes about four days to ice the entire track to build up enough ice so that sleds

260
00:20:37,201 --> 00:20:42,680
can go down without the runners hitting the concrete.

261
00:20:42,681 --> 00:20:46,460
Once completed, the Whistler Sliding Center created instant buzz in the sport.

262
00:20:47,660 --> 00:20:51,400
And within months, world record speeds in bobsled were already being shattered.

263
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:56,560
The lightning speed comes from the track's 500-foot drop in elevation.

264
00:20:57,560 --> 00:21:00,220
And it's 16 treacherous hairpin turns.

265
00:21:01,840 --> 00:21:06,120
The toughest turn, Curve 13, has earned the nickname 50-50.

266
00:21:07,020 --> 00:21:11,200
Meaning athletes have a 50-50 chance of making it through without crashing.

267
00:21:11,960 --> 00:21:14,420
The more speed we can put on there, I think the athletes are happier.

268
00:21:15,080 --> 00:21:16,400
We are pushing them to the limit.

269
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:19,080
I think they push themselves to the limit more so than we can.

270
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:22,700
That's the pride that they take, being the fastest and the best.

271
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:28,500
But the fastest human-powered sport of the Games

272
00:21:28,501 --> 00:21:32,320
is taking place just south of Vancouver in the city of Richmond.

273
00:21:33,480 --> 00:21:35,480
It's home to long-track speed skating,

274
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:38,000
and this is no ordinary ice rink.

275
00:21:38,001 --> 00:21:42,000
The Richmond Olympic Oval is definitely going to be a gem of the games this year in 2010.

276
00:21:42,460 --> 00:21:45,480
Just the grand size of it is overwhelming.

277
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:49,720
If this particular building was downtown, it would be two blocks long by two blocks wide.

278
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:55,860
An empty plot was transformed over two years into the $168 million venue.

279
00:21:57,440 --> 00:22:03,000
The massive structure's 6.5-acre roof is large enough to house four jetliners.

280
00:22:03,001 --> 00:22:08,440
Aside from its stunning profile, the building is a lean green design.

281
00:22:08,441 --> 00:22:15,760
The structure has a wooden wave-shaped roof, which redirects rainwater day and night for

282
00:22:15,761 --> 00:22:17,760
use in the rink's ice.

283
00:22:17,761 --> 00:22:21,580
The water in front of the building is a reservoir that's been collected of all the water coming

284
00:22:21,581 --> 00:22:22,580
off of our roof.

285
00:22:22,581 --> 00:22:26,000
It's collected in the pond, and then once in the pond it's circulated through a filtration

286
00:22:26,001 --> 00:22:27,000
system.

287
00:22:27,001 --> 00:22:33,720
Once filtered, high-tech pumps under the building turn 3,000 gallons of water into Olympic-grade ice.

288
00:22:34,960 --> 00:22:43,220
But unlike the uniform ice of the bobsled track, officials hear vary icing conditions between the curves and the straightaways.

289
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:46,940
What we're looking at here is one of the curves at the Richmond Olympic Oval,

290
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:53,080
where we have the ability to actually make this curve a little bit warmer in temperature as opposed to the straight stretches.

291
00:22:53,081 --> 00:22:58,220
This allows the athletes to bite in and maintain those fast speeds that they're taking in a corner.

292
00:23:00,220 --> 00:23:05,880
The floor's cooling system keeps ice on the straightaways colder and harder to help skaters accelerate.

293
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:11,040
It's all to make up for the Oval's low altitude, a problem in speed skating.

294
00:23:12,360 --> 00:23:16,320
Unlike the record-setting high-altitude tracks in Calgary and Salt Lake,

295
00:23:17,060 --> 00:23:18,960
Richmond's venue is expected to be slower,

296
00:23:19,080 --> 00:23:22,820
since the lower altitude means thicker air for the athletes to skate through.

297
00:23:23,080 --> 00:23:28,780
And Richmond's humid coastal air presents another challenge, moisture.

298
00:23:29,280 --> 00:23:32,680
The humidity level is really important because we don't want to have frost build up on the ice.

299
00:23:32,681 --> 00:23:37,780
If we have moisture in the air and that moist air hits the cold ice, it's going to build up frost on the ice.

300
00:23:38,140 --> 00:23:39,940
Frost on the ice means slower times.

301
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:45,840
It means more ice cleans are going to have to be done in order to resurface the ice and have it ready for the athletes to skate on.

302
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:52,200
Engineers have also installed a computer-controlled air handling system to help dry out the building.

303
00:23:52,201 --> 00:23:55,140
with special dehumidifiers built into the ceiling.

304
00:23:55,920 --> 00:23:57,600
All the air is being pushed out of the building,

305
00:23:57,780 --> 00:24:01,860
so because of that, we're able to hopefully maintain our 32% to 38% humidity

306
00:24:01,861 --> 00:24:06,500
and keep all of that really humid air outside and not inside the building.

307
00:24:07,140 --> 00:24:10,000
With its high-tech features and eye-catching design,

308
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:15,240
the Richmond Oval will be the perfect venue for nail-biting races and capacity crowds.

309
00:24:16,680 --> 00:24:19,840
Speed skaters have already taken to the ice at the Richmond Oval

310
00:24:19,841 --> 00:24:22,300
to prepare for the 2010 Winter Games.

311
00:24:23,100 --> 00:24:26,400
But athletes also need to train year-round for outdoor events.

312
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:30,660
It's winter sports without ice or snow.

313
00:24:33,100 --> 00:24:35,440
The sport of bobsled got its name

314
00:24:35,441 --> 00:24:38,020
from the way early teams would bob their heads

315
00:24:38,021 --> 00:24:40,820
to gain more speed on straight portions of a run.

316
00:24:42,340 --> 00:24:45,240
Winter Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

317
00:24:49,840 --> 00:24:55,200
The Vancouver 2010 Olympic Winter Games are the ultimate test for cold weather athletes.

318
00:24:56,700 --> 00:25:01,140
Imagine rocketing down a winding sheet of ice at close to 100 miles an hour

319
00:25:01,141 --> 00:25:04,020
with no more protection than a helmet.

320
00:25:04,680 --> 00:25:08,940
For athletes competing in luge, it's just another day on the job.

321
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:16,360
And like many winter athletes, they rely on cutting-edge tools to get them ready for competition season.

322
00:25:19,840 --> 00:25:23,520
At the U.S. Luge Center in Lake Placid, New York,

323
00:25:24,120 --> 00:25:27,840
U.S. Junior Luge team members train on a high-tech indoor track.

324
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:30,480
This is our indoor refrigerated start ramp.

325
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:33,740
Luge is a winter sport, and we only get a chance to slide

326
00:25:33,741 --> 00:25:35,540
from about October to March,

327
00:25:35,960 --> 00:25:39,220
and this gives us an opportunity to train in the summertime.

328
00:25:40,560 --> 00:25:43,620
Here, technology also helps luge athletes

329
00:25:43,621 --> 00:25:46,040
hone in on the sport's most critical moment.

330
00:25:46,041 --> 00:25:54,880
the luge star is your one chance to accelerate the sled once you pull off those handles and

331
00:25:54,881 --> 00:25:59,500
paddle down the ramp and settle down into the sled you're just trying to build speed and gravity

332
00:25:59,501 --> 00:26:05,680
takes over every thousandth of a second that you can gain when you pull off the handles is very

333
00:26:05,681 --> 00:26:10,020
very important for the rest of the run if you're behind on the start there's no real way to make

334
00:26:10,021 --> 00:26:16,980
that up. Sensors measure each athlete's grip on the luge handles and the power of

335
00:26:16,981 --> 00:26:22,780
their release. It's down to a thousandth of a second. It's way too way too close

336
00:26:22,781 --> 00:26:26,520
when you're coming down to a race and you lose by two thousandths of a second

337
00:26:26,521 --> 00:26:31,740
and you know there goes your gold medal. Each practice start is recorded and

338
00:26:31,741 --> 00:26:37,780
analyzed down to the frame. What's your extension heading into this third paddle?

339
00:26:37,781 --> 00:26:43,700
We have four different cameras that can record all sorts of angles of athletes pulling the

340
00:26:43,701 --> 00:26:44,700
start.

341
00:26:44,701 --> 00:26:52,540
At 90 mile per hour speeds, athletes are limited to just a few trials a day.

342
00:26:52,541 --> 00:26:57,500
The sled is about 50 pounds, so they're pulling a lot of weight down the track with them.

343
00:26:57,501 --> 00:27:01,800
Headaches will happen, and just the general vibration really, really starts to hurt your

344
00:27:01,801 --> 00:27:02,800
body.

345
00:27:02,801 --> 00:27:05,760
While the luge team tests the limits of the human body,

346
00:27:07,280 --> 00:27:10,340
ski jumpers test the very boundaries of physics.

347
00:27:14,780 --> 00:27:17,520
Most of us fantasize about flying.

348
00:27:19,140 --> 00:27:21,000
In Lake Placid, New York,

349
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:24,260
U.S. ski jumpers know exactly what we're missing.

350
00:27:30,500 --> 00:27:32,600
I love the feeling of flying.

351
00:27:32,601 --> 00:27:34,900
It is the most awesome feeling in the world.

352
00:27:35,420 --> 00:27:39,160
Just being able to come off and flying through the air,

353
00:27:39,240 --> 00:27:42,900
have the wind flow through your suit by your body and underneath your skis.

354
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:47,840
It's just awesome.

355
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:53,840
In Ski Jump, high-flying Olympians are judged based on their distance and style,

356
00:27:54,340 --> 00:27:57,580
with record flights stretching close to 800 feet.

357
00:27:58,160 --> 00:28:01,100
That's like jumping across two-and-a-half football fields.

358
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:09,600
The Olympic ski jump hill in Lake Placid is outfitted for both winter and summer jumps.

359
00:28:09,601 --> 00:28:17,600
The jump slope, or in-run, is equipped with plastic tracks.

360
00:28:17,601 --> 00:28:21,600
In the summer, these tracks are watered down to minimize resistance.

361
00:28:21,601 --> 00:28:30,600
The landing area, normally cushioned by a thick layer of snow, gets a customized artificial surface.

362
00:28:30,601 --> 00:28:34,500
This big, weird area of green that you see behind me

363
00:28:34,501 --> 00:28:38,020
and that I'm actually kneeling on, this is plastic.

364
00:28:38,260 --> 00:28:42,420
It's the artificial surface that we use to ski jump in the summertime.

365
00:28:43,300 --> 00:28:46,660
It kind of looks like uncooked spaghetti, if you ask me.

366
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:51,800
There's actually a rubber pad that runs all the way up under there

367
00:28:51,801 --> 00:28:53,480
to cushion it a little bit.

368
00:28:54,020 --> 00:28:59,220
And once you put water on this, it is essentially smoother than snow.

369
00:29:00,600 --> 00:29:05,240
Ski jumpers strive for a relaxed, zen-like state through constant repetition.

370
00:29:05,880 --> 00:29:09,720
The biggest thing that we work on is how we go off the end of the jump.

371
00:29:10,020 --> 00:29:13,760
We want to stay really relaxed with our upper body, try not to be really tense.

372
00:29:16,320 --> 00:29:20,420
Just like the Alpine team, ski jumpers practice in high-tech wind tunnels.

373
00:29:21,020 --> 00:29:24,520
It's very helpful because if you're in a wind tunnel testing your flying position,

374
00:29:24,660 --> 00:29:27,320
as we see here, watching them fly is pretty spectacular,

375
00:29:27,321 --> 00:29:29,180
but they're only doing it for three, four, five seconds,

376
00:29:29,181 --> 00:29:32,380
whereas in the wind tunnel you can do it for five or six, seven minutes at a time.

377
00:29:32,700 --> 00:29:34,700
I mean, that's years' worth of jumping, really.

378
00:29:34,980 --> 00:29:38,040
That's thousands of jumps in comparison to what you would get on a daily basis,

379
00:29:38,180 --> 00:29:39,300
so it's very helpful.

380
00:29:41,420 --> 00:29:44,380
Flying with skis requires a lot of technology.

381
00:29:45,280 --> 00:29:46,720
The skis are custom-crafted,

382
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:51,120
and the synthetic suit and helmet are designed for maximum aerodynamics.

383
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:56,940
If you look at the athlete and you look at his body and the equipment that he wears,

384
00:29:56,941 --> 00:30:00,460
it's probably more inspected than a NASCAR race car.

385
00:30:01,660 --> 00:30:04,280
The suit is controlled, the size of the ski,

386
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:06,380
the binding placement is all controlled,

387
00:30:06,500 --> 00:30:09,640
and they are tested constantly throughout a competition weekend

388
00:30:09,641 --> 00:30:14,360
to make sure, because a 1% manipulation of an oversizing of a suit

389
00:30:14,361 --> 00:30:19,300
is a 1% gain, and that 1% is enough to win a gold medal still today.

390
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:23,560
And the jumps themselves are engineered down to the millimeter.

391
00:30:26,940 --> 00:30:31,160
For Whistler's Olympic ski jumps, engineers had to reshape a mountainside

392
00:30:31,161 --> 00:30:35,100
by clearing out over 3.5 million cubic feet of earth.

393
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:37,820
This is just a forested hillside.

394
00:30:38,020 --> 00:30:41,380
We basically had to take all the trees off, drill and blast rock,

395
00:30:41,880 --> 00:30:45,100
move dirt from the top down to the bottom, and truck it out of here.

396
00:30:46,840 --> 00:30:53,080
The support structures for the monster jumps required 140,000 cubic feet of concrete

397
00:30:53,081 --> 00:30:57,060
and over 110,000 pounds of reinforcing steel.

398
00:30:59,180 --> 00:31:04,420
The jump's in run is designed at an exact 35-degree slope,

399
00:31:04,720 --> 00:31:08,480
which the landing hill must mimic to follow the jumper's flight path.

400
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,500
The takeoff point isn't a jump at all.

401
00:31:12,160 --> 00:31:16,580
It's 11 degrees below horizontal to aid the jumper in a safe landing.

402
00:31:16,581 --> 00:31:26,040
after a year of construction the whistler ski jump is ready for the world stage

403
00:31:26,041 --> 00:31:33,460
the setting is absolutely fantastic here the views the valley you get glaciers in the background and

404
00:31:33,461 --> 00:31:40,220
it's just a fantastic venue and i think the world will love it this olympics fans can get around

405
00:31:40,221 --> 00:31:43,060
while suspended a quarter mile above the valley floor.

406
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:46,660
Climb on board one of the world's highest ski lifts

407
00:31:46,661 --> 00:31:48,320
on Modern Marvels.

408
00:31:50,260 --> 00:31:53,820
In 1985, Swedish ski jumper Jan Baklev

409
00:31:53,821 --> 00:31:56,420
spread the tips of his skis into a V formation

410
00:31:56,421 --> 00:31:58,760
and found it improved his aerodynamics.

411
00:31:59,300 --> 00:32:02,280
While initially ridiculed, the technique proved successful,

412
00:32:02,281 --> 00:32:06,320
and by 1992, all Olympic medalists had adopted it.

413
00:32:10,220 --> 00:32:17,700
Vancouver is the largest city ever to host the Winter Olympics.

414
00:32:18,540 --> 00:32:21,060
Extra trains and ferries are being added

415
00:32:21,061 --> 00:32:25,100
to handle the million passengers a day expected on public transit.

416
00:32:27,100 --> 00:32:30,100
But the most exciting way for spectators to get around

417
00:32:30,101 --> 00:32:32,440
sits atop Whistler's towering peaks.

418
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:36,700
The peak-to-peak gondola is absolutely a unique lift.

419
00:32:36,701 --> 00:32:40,400
It spans the largest unsupported distance in the world.

420
00:32:40,900 --> 00:32:46,640
It's also the highest, which makes Whistler Blackcomb the largest continuous lift system in the world.

421
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:51,560
The Peak-to-Peak Gondola is a record-breaking engineering achievement.

422
00:32:52,480 --> 00:32:57,100
It has the longest unsupported gap of any gondola, stretching almost two miles.

423
00:32:58,440 --> 00:33:02,980
And it dangles its passengers more than 1,400 feet above the valley floor,

424
00:33:04,100 --> 00:33:06,060
higher than the Empire State Building.

425
00:33:06,700 --> 00:33:11,280
It's become a must-see when you come to Whistler.

426
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:13,020
This is an unbelievable ride.

427
00:33:15,300 --> 00:33:18,320
What did it take to build this sky-high ski lift?

428
00:33:20,460 --> 00:33:23,300
With a deep valley separating the two peaks,

429
00:33:23,960 --> 00:33:26,800
engineers designed massive 200-foot towers,

430
00:33:27,840 --> 00:33:29,480
strong enough to anchor each end.

431
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:34,260
After blasting rock and pouring concrete foundations,

432
00:33:34,261 --> 00:33:40,500
Some of the world's top free climbers were recruited to help erect the two steel towers.

433
00:33:43,280 --> 00:33:48,700
But with an almost two-mile unsupported gap and 3,000-pound gondolas,

434
00:33:49,140 --> 00:33:50,980
they were going to need a whole lot of cable.

435
00:33:52,420 --> 00:33:58,360
Five giant spools with a combined weight of 342 tons were shipped from Switzerland.

436
00:33:58,361 --> 00:34:06,360
Carrying the spools required special trucks with 12 independently rotating axles to navigate the

437
00:34:06,361 --> 00:34:07,847
windy mountain roads.

438
00:34:07,848 --> 00:34:12,660
It took three months to close the 2.7 mile gap between the completed

439
00:34:12,661 --> 00:34:18,562
towers and now this engineering marvel is ready for the crowds.

440
00:34:18,563 --> 00:34:21,560
Meanwhile Olympic fans will have

441
00:34:21,561 --> 00:34:25,280
plenty to do back in Vancouver, even when they're not at the events.

442
00:34:26,520 --> 00:34:31,860
A 7,500 square foot pavilion is taking shape just for visitors to the Games.

443
00:34:32,460 --> 00:34:37,060
It's called the Olympic Rendezvous at Samsung, or the ORS.

444
00:34:37,520 --> 00:34:40,600
It's coming to life, so we're all excited.

445
00:34:40,800 --> 00:34:44,500
There's still a lot of work to do, but it's finally coming together.

446
00:34:47,300 --> 00:34:50,660
The ORS is an interactive hub for fans and athletes,

447
00:34:50,661 --> 00:34:56,400
Using the latest wireless technology, the ORS will connect them with everything they need to know about the Games.

448
00:34:56,980 --> 00:35:01,400
Built by Samsung, worldwide Olympic partner in wireless telecommunication equipment,

449
00:35:01,680 --> 00:35:07,500
the ORS made a splash at the 2008 Olympic Summer Games in Beijing, its fifth appearance at an Olympic Games.

450
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:11,420
Our former chairman, Kenny Lee, had a vision for Samsung.

451
00:35:12,420 --> 00:35:19,920
The Olympic Games would allow the world to get to know Samsung, our value, and our commitment to society.

452
00:35:19,921 --> 00:35:25,920
The Rendezvous in Vancouver expects to host over 600,000 people.

453
00:35:25,921 --> 00:35:29,920
It will provide a sneak peek into the latest mobile technology

454
00:35:29,921 --> 00:35:32,920
and provide a convenient place to gather.

455
00:35:32,921 --> 00:35:38,920
You won't even have to be at the Olympics to have real-time access to all the latest updates from the Games.

456
00:35:38,921 --> 00:35:44,920
Samsung is providing its users around the world with its Wireless Olympic Works, or WOW.

457
00:35:44,921 --> 00:35:48,920
There's so much going on that it's hard to keep up to date with all that,

458
00:35:48,921 --> 00:35:55,120
So WOW is really a one-stop shop for anybody to go and get up-to-date information about the Olympics.

459
00:35:55,480 --> 00:35:58,620
And it doesn't matter where you are, it's anywhere in the world.

460
00:35:59,460 --> 00:36:05,000
Another first for the 2010 Winter Games, keeping all those cell phones charged and ready to use.

461
00:36:06,420 --> 00:36:12,200
More than 70 specially designed charging stations have been placed at strategic locations around the Games.

462
00:36:12,740 --> 00:36:15,440
Just leave your battery and you can come back for it later.

463
00:36:15,441 --> 00:36:20,020
Usually it would take up to three hours to charge your cell phone battery.

464
00:36:20,500 --> 00:36:24,020
If you use the accelerated charger, it would take up to 30 minutes.

465
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:29,320
It's a very fast and secure way to keep yourself charged during the Games.

466
00:36:31,200 --> 00:36:35,580
A unique PIN number keeps the battery safe until it's retrieved.

467
00:36:36,780 --> 00:36:43,380
We hope to convey that Samsung is genuinely committed to the values and ideals of Olympic Games,

468
00:36:43,381 --> 00:36:46,680
including global friendship and corporate citizenship.

469
00:36:46,681 --> 00:36:51,680
Samsung wanted to connect the people of the world to the Olympic Games.

470
00:36:51,681 --> 00:36:55,680
To do this, we offer our best wireless technology.

471
00:36:55,681 --> 00:37:00,680
From transportation to handheld communications,

472
00:37:00,681 --> 00:37:06,680
technology is keeping fans connected to the 2010 Winter Games more than ever.

473
00:37:06,681 --> 00:37:11,680
But there's a secret location where no fan is allowed.

474
00:37:11,681 --> 00:37:15,480
where engineers design the most iconic symbol of any Olympic Games,

475
00:37:16,980 --> 00:37:18,700
the Olympic torch.

476
00:37:20,620 --> 00:37:23,440
This telecast is presented by History

477
00:37:23,441 --> 00:37:26,840
in association with the United States Olympic Committee.

478
00:37:33,660 --> 00:37:38,120
Technology is front and center at the Vancouver 2010 Olympic Winter Games.

479
00:37:38,121 --> 00:37:42,460
from the venues to cutting-edge gear,

480
00:37:43,280 --> 00:37:48,340
even down to the iconic Olympic torch.

481
00:37:52,160 --> 00:37:55,980
For every Olympic Games, the torch is re-engineered for extremes

482
00:37:55,981 --> 00:38:00,840
from the peaks of Mount Everest to the bottom of the sea.

483
00:38:01,620 --> 00:38:03,600
And this year's torch is no exception.

484
00:38:04,560 --> 00:38:07,000
Canada is the second largest country in the world,

485
00:38:07,001 --> 00:38:16,240
So the torch had to be specially engineered for the longest single-country torch relay in Olympic history, over 28,000 miles.

486
00:38:18,300 --> 00:38:21,260
For Olympic organizers, it's a unique challenge.

487
00:38:22,280 --> 00:38:26,480
They need to sustain a flame for 106 days through Canada's varied terrain,

488
00:38:28,720 --> 00:38:33,480
including the northernmost inhabited spot on Earth, just 500 miles from the North Pole.

489
00:38:33,481 --> 00:38:40,480
The torch must withstand temperatures as low as 40 degrees below zero.

490
00:38:40,481 --> 00:38:46,480
To take on the task, organizers called on Bombardier Global Transportation Company,

491
00:38:46,481 --> 00:38:50,480
Canada's leading aerospace and railway designer.

492
00:38:50,481 --> 00:38:57,480
We had to produce in two years 12,000 units, but actually just by the time we start to design it,

493
00:38:57,481 --> 00:39:02,700
design it can make the style find the processes the materials and end up we

494
00:39:02,701 --> 00:39:09,840
have six months to manufacture 2000 torch in a secret location in Montreal

495
00:39:09,841 --> 00:39:16,540
two shifts of workers assembled torches around the clock with only six months to

496
00:39:16,541 --> 00:39:22,540
put them together the torches were designed with ease of assembly in mind

497
00:39:22,541 --> 00:39:26,640
Building a weatherproof flame starts with hardware.

498
00:39:26,641 --> 00:39:36,220
The working components of the torch are a lot like a camping stove, a valve, regulator,

499
00:39:36,221 --> 00:39:43,080
and fuel line connect to an evaporation tube and a burner tube.

500
00:39:43,081 --> 00:39:47,500
Another compartment houses a pilot burner to keep the flame from going out in Canada's

501
00:39:47,501 --> 00:39:50,480
extreme weather.

502
00:39:50,481 --> 00:39:57,160
If the wind knocks out the main flame, the pilot burner will instantly relight the torch.

503
00:39:57,161 --> 00:40:01,540
And every fire needs fuel.

504
00:40:01,541 --> 00:40:08,980
In past torches, magnesium, gunpowder, resin, and even olive oil have been used.

505
00:40:08,981 --> 00:40:13,880
This year's torch uses a durable mix of fuel.

506
00:40:13,881 --> 00:40:18,480
It's a blend of propane and isobutane.

507
00:40:18,481 --> 00:40:20,740
The isobutin gives us a really nice colored flame,

508
00:40:21,100 --> 00:40:24,040
and the propane is really good for cold weather.

509
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:27,440
So by mixing both, we came up with a really nice flame,

510
00:40:27,900 --> 00:40:30,060
colored, that doesn't produce any smoke,

511
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:32,520
and it could send the weather outside.

512
00:40:34,260 --> 00:40:35,880
With the hardware in place,

513
00:40:36,140 --> 00:40:39,260
designers went a step further to reshape the flame itself.

514
00:40:41,940 --> 00:40:44,780
Historically, the Olympic torch has featured a flame

515
00:40:44,781 --> 00:40:46,800
that sits on top of the torch.

516
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:51,180
This year, the flame comes out three sides.

517
00:40:52,580 --> 00:40:55,540
You've got the burner actually sitting like this on the torch,

518
00:40:55,700 --> 00:40:58,400
and the flame will go out from actually the bottom here

519
00:40:58,401 --> 00:41:01,120
up to the top and actually a bit forward.

520
00:41:01,900 --> 00:41:04,000
So you've got actually a really nice and long flame.

521
00:41:05,500 --> 00:41:06,520
When you're going to run with it,

522
00:41:06,620 --> 00:41:09,260
it's actually doing like a flag, like a protrude out.

523
00:41:12,420 --> 00:41:16,140
And all of this has to fit in a body light enough to carry.

524
00:41:18,480 --> 00:41:28,520
The nearly three-foot-long torch is made of stainless steel, anodized aluminum, and composite material, weighing a mere three-and-a-half pounds.

525
00:41:29,960 --> 00:41:34,640
Workers assemble each torch by hand and give it a personalized signature.

526
00:41:36,020 --> 00:41:38,880
But each torch has to do more than carry a flame.

527
00:41:40,720 --> 00:41:48,260
It also has to symbolize the spirit of the 2010 Winter Games, while featuring some unique Canadian touches.

528
00:41:48,480 --> 00:41:54,320
Designers found special inspiration in the winter sports themselves.

529
00:41:55,700 --> 00:41:59,000
We wanted to create something that almost looked like it was a windblown object.

530
00:41:59,360 --> 00:42:01,920
So we've got these twists that you can see on the side.

531
00:42:02,260 --> 00:42:05,820
So it looks almost like the ridge that you get at the top of a snow drift.

532
00:42:07,280 --> 00:42:09,480
Similarly, we wanted to get this stainless steel edge,

533
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:13,580
which sort of symbolizes the edge of a ski or a skate plate.

534
00:42:15,020 --> 00:42:16,420
Obviously, it's a once-in-a-lifetime thing.

535
00:42:16,460 --> 00:42:17,920
It's a dream project for any designer.

536
00:42:17,921 --> 00:42:22,460
You don't expect to be able to have the opportunity to be involved in something like this.

537
00:42:34,940 --> 00:42:39,320
Competing in the Winter Olympic Games is a dream come true for any winter athlete.

538
00:42:42,340 --> 00:42:46,400
But without behind-the-scenes technology, none of it would be possible.

539
00:42:47,920 --> 00:42:50,300
Snow teams shaping white mountainsides.

540
00:42:51,760 --> 00:42:53,640
Engineers prepping icy raceways.

541
00:42:55,880 --> 00:42:59,940
And fans connecting to the competition in new and exciting ways.

542
00:43:01,580 --> 00:43:06,800
And the next generation of Olympic hopefuls continues to use technology to turn tighter.

543
00:43:09,060 --> 00:43:10,400
Slide faster.

544
00:43:12,280 --> 00:43:15,480
And fly farther than ever before.

